WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
We know that the order is four u squared.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.340
Separate the power of two in u by
writing u equals two to the s minus one,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.540
times w, where s is at
least one and w is odd.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.780
Substituting this into the order gives n
equals two to the two s, times w squared.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.920
We will keep the power
of two as a cycle length,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.600
and collect the odd part
into the coefficients.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.267
Reduce every exponent in h
of X modulo two to the two s.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.280
To picture the grouping, consider the
hypothetical order thirty-six case.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.110
Here u is three, s is one, and w is three.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.144
The shorter cycle has four
positions. The positions zero,

00:00:42.556 --> 00:00:46.940
four, eight, and so on through
thirty-two all arrive at residue zero.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.280
Each of the other three residues
also collects nine positions.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.700
These are symbolic signs
from a hypothetical row;

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.400
we have not constructed an order
thirty-six Hadamard matrix.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.250
In general, the coefficient a sub
r is the sum of h sub r plus ell

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.150
times two to the two s, as ell runs
from zero to w squared minus one.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.380
Call the new polynomial T of X.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.420
Each new coefficient is a sum
of exactly w squared signs.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.620
Modulo two, both signs are one. An odd
number of them therefore has odd sum.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.440
So every a sub r is an odd integer,

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.880
although it need not still
be plus or minus one.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.600
Why does the product identity survive?

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.460
The shorter cycle length divides
n. Reducing exponents is therefore

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.740
a ring map: it respects
addition and multiplication.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.740
It also sends the inverse of X to the
inverse of its image, so it respects star.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.060
Thus h maps to T, and
h star maps to T star.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.460
Apply this same map to
h times h star equals n.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.447
We obtain T times T star
equals two to the two s,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.460
times w squared, in the integer group
ring of the shorter cycle. The number

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.140
of positions has decreased, but the scalar
on the right has stayed exactly the same.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.360
We now carry two facts forward:
two to the two s odd coefficients,

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.640
and this unchanged scalar norm.
If s is at least two, the next

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.360
steps compare opposite coefficients
and repeatedly fold the cycle.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.280
Their contradiction will
force s to equal one.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:30.380
In the original notation, that
will say u equals w and is odd.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.280
For now, we have prepared the shorter
cycle on which that descent can work.
